course: dictionary
lesson: 5058
title: Inseparable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Inseparable is an adjective. It describes two things, or two people, that are always together. «At riding lessons she meets Kenzie, who has a pony-shaped pencil case and zero shame, and by spring they're inseparable.» The clue is «by spring»: after a few months together, they are never far apart. So inseparable means always together.
explain: The word can also mean impossible to separate. Two ideas can be inseparable if you cannot think about one without the other. Look at the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: Inseparable has an adverb too: inseparably. It tells you how things happen, as in «they were inseparably linked».
fact: Inseparable means always together, or impossible to separate.
fact: An adjective describes a noun, as in «inseparable friends».
fact: The adverb of inseparable is inseparably.
fact: Phrasal verbs can be inseparable: you say «look after the baby», not «look the baby after».
fact: In science, some mixtures or parts are not easy to separate.
exercise: «At riding lessons she meets Kenzie, and by spring they're inseparable.» What does inseparable mean here? | *Always together, close friends | Angry with each other | Good at riding horses | Far apart and never meeting
exercise: What is the adverb form of inseparable? | separately | *inseparably | inseparate | inseparated
exercise: Which sentence keeps the phrasal verb together correctly? | Mum will look the baby after | Mum will after the baby look | *Mum will look after the baby
test: Mia and her gran do everything together. They are ___. | invisible | separate | impossible to find | *inseparable
test: «The two ideas are inseparable.» What does this mean? | Hard to spell | *Impossible to separate | Very loud | Always late
exam: In «Kenzie and I are inseparable», what kind of word is inseparable? | noun | verb | *adjective | adverb
